WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
TWILIO:
By now, I think we’re all familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:09.040
with one time passcodes. They’re the pesky but&nbsp;
required 6 digit codes that enable two factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.000
authentication and keep all of our accounts secure&nbsp;
from hackers and bots. Most of us have likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.120
never given OTPs much thought, it’s just a normal&nbsp;
part of the modern tech world but it’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:27.680
a massive industry supporting several massive&nbsp;
players. You of course have your classic tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.360
companies including Google’s Identity Platform and&nbsp;
Amazon’s Cognito. You’ve probably also heard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:40.640
Duo Mobile and Octa but have you ever heard of a&nbsp;
company called Twilio. Twilio is arguably the king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:48.400
of automated text messages and emails with a peak&nbsp;
market cap of over $70 billion. This means that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.840
their peak, they were one of the top 250 largest&nbsp;
companies in the world. But, more recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:01.840
things haven’t been looking as bright for this&nbsp;
OTP giant. In fact, Twilio stock has fallen 85%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.080
bringing their market cap all they way down to&nbsp;
just over $12 billion. It’s easy to write off this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:13.280
decline as just a byproduct of the volatile market&nbsp;
and recession fears but there seems to be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:19.440
to Twilio’s story. Not only did they fall much&nbsp;
further than big tech but they have yet to see any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.280
sort of recovery and when you take a look at their&nbsp;
financial reports it all starts to make sense. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.680
see, Twilio has never been a profitable company,&nbsp;
having relied on VC funding and stock dilution to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.960
keep the business running. This strategy works out&nbsp;
when revenue is growing exponentially but not so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.920
much when revenue starts to stall out and that’s&nbsp;
exactly what’s happening to Twilio whose is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:48.800
much stalling out at the $4 billion mark. To make&nbsp;
things worse, it doesn’t seem like their expenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.240
are getting any better meaning that Twilio is&nbsp;
currently burning well over a billion every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.240 --> 00:02:00.000
single year. With only $3.5 billion left in the&nbsp;
bank, this is not exactly an ideal situation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.920
the company and it’s no wonder why investors are&nbsp;
staying away. So, join me as we take a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:20.498
rise and fall of the king of OTPs and whether&nbsp;
Twilio will ever return to their former glory.

00:02:20.498 --> 00:02:22.600
AN UNDERSERVED MARKET:
Taking a look back, Twilio didn’t start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:29.840
off as an OTP company because well OTPs weren’t&nbsp;
even a thing when Twilio was founded in 2007. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.400
main guy behind Twilio was a software developer&nbsp;
and serial entrepreneur named Jeff Lawson. Jeff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.560
was a former AWS product manager who was obsessed&nbsp;
with perfecting user experiences. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:47.000
Jeff strongly believes that this is the main job&nbsp;
of software engineers, to iteratively understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:53.120
customers and build better and better solutions&nbsp;
using software. But despite founding 3 companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.040
before Twilio, Jeff couldn’t help but feel that&nbsp;
he was constantly falling short in this area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.600
and to him, the reason was obvious, a lack of&nbsp;
communication. With traditional businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.520
whether its a retail store or a restaurant,&nbsp;
you’re constantly able to interact with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.840
customers and get a feel for what they like, what&nbsp;
they don’t like, and what could be improved. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.040
with software, while you’re able to reach&nbsp;
more customers than any traditional business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.360
you’re also far more disconnected from your&nbsp;
customers. Aside from a random review that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.560
might pop up from time to time, there was really&nbsp;
no way to directly communicate with customers. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.800
Jeff started looking into ways that he could setup&nbsp;
communication infrastructure himself which is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:42.880
he ran into a massive reality check. It turns out&nbsp;
that building out a communications platform is far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.880
harder than building out a software platform.&nbsp;
According to Jeff himself, he was used to used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.880
to software development timeslines in the weeks.&nbsp;
Building out this communication infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:59.680
however, would take 2 to 3 years and $2 to&nbsp;
$3 million. Put off by the cost and timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.920
Jeff would start looking into big tech solutions&nbsp;
which is when he realized that there was none,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.560
and that’s when it hit him. If he needed to&nbsp;
communicate with customers, it’s likely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.920
other developers did as well. And if there&nbsp;
was no established solution on the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:21.200
then maybe he could be that solution. And with&nbsp;
that, we saw the birth of Twilio: a company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.560
was going to make communications between software&nbsp;
companies and users easier than ever. The journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:33.600
to build this company, however, was by no means&nbsp;
easy. The problem was that Jeff needed VC money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.000
and no VC really understood what he was trying to&nbsp;
do. In fact, Jeff started off with what he deemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.200
to be a safe pitch, a pitch to a person that&nbsp;
he knew pretty well. But this person would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.840
up laughing Jeff out of the room asserting that&nbsp;
Jeff would need an insane amount of sales people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.480
to get this off the ground. This disappointing&nbsp;
outcome made it clear to Jeff that he needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:00.280
proof before he approached VCs, so instead of&nbsp;
pitching, he would get to work. He would build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.840
a voice API that was completely hosted on the&nbsp;
cloud and could make and receive phone calls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:10.960
and they would launch it on November 20, 2008.&nbsp;
Keep in mind, this was a couple months before even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.560
Google Voice came out, so Twilio was very much&nbsp;
early to the game. And as such, they were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.320
to land some pretty big clients pretty quickly.&nbsp;
This included the likes of Cheetos, Earth911,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.760
Tumblr, and Sony Music. It turned out that Twilio&nbsp;
Voice was also super helpful for politicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.000
who wanted to automate the process of calling&nbsp;
voters. With these customers in his back pocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:48.546
Jeff would return to the VC table and let’s just&nbsp;
say, he would be taken a lot more seriously.

00:05:48.546 --> 00:05:51.480
GROWING PAINS:
In early 2009, Twilio would complete a seed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:57.280
funding round raising $600,000. If you’re familiar&nbsp;
with Chris Sacca from Shark Tank, he was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.280
Twilio’s seed investors. They would follow this up&nbsp;
with a Series A round of funding just a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:08.720
months later raising $3.7 million. Now that Twilio&nbsp;
had money, Jeff would double down on his sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:14.680
effort. According to Jeff, the key to growing&nbsp;
Twilio was treating developers like customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:19.880
You see, a lot of enterprise companies get into&nbsp;
the habit of treating developers as second tier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:25.240
customers, and it’s no wonder why. When you’re&nbsp;
serving developers, considerations like public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.200
reputation and brand image aren’t nearly as&nbsp;
important. Not to mention, there’s only a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.400
of developer solutions for any given market, so&nbsp;
developers aren’t that likely to switch to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.240
service provider in the first place. So, companies&nbsp;
often tend to treat developers as just a strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:48.360
to reach more end users and make their experience&nbsp;
even better. As a software developer, Jeff was all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:54.600
too aware of this reality and he wanted Twilio to&nbsp;
be different. He wanted Twillio to put developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:00.760
first and treat developers as customers and this&nbsp;
has really been their strategy. They would sponsor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.640
hackathons, run hackathons, and hand out a bunch&nbsp;
of shirts to developers. In fact, at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.320
Jeff would make the prediction that Twilio would&nbsp;
hand out more shirts to developers than Blackberry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:16.640
would ship phones. Looking back, this wasn’t&nbsp;
too hard given that Blackberry would fall off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:23.080
a cliff but I think you get the idea, Twilio was&nbsp;
reaching a lot of developers. As their reach grew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.920
Twilio also focused on expanding their product&nbsp;
line which led to the creation of their text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:34.760
messaging API in February of 2010. Originally,&nbsp;
this wasn’t meant for OTPs. Rather, it was meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:40.120
for updating customers. For example, messages like&nbsp;
“Your shipment is on the way” or “Your package is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.560
out for delivery”. It wasn’t till mid 2011 that&nbsp;
Twilio launched their shortcode API platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.480
and this is when all of Jeff’s original worries&nbsp;
came back to life. With their voice API and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:59.400
original SMS api, infrastructure wasn’t too big of&nbsp;
a concern because the volume wasn’t too large. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:06.840
with OTPs, companies would be sending millions if&nbsp;
tens of millions of messages on a daily daily. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.120
not only did Twilio need strong infrastructure but&nbsp;
they also needed rock solid deals with carriers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.960
to process and send so many text messages. Twilio&nbsp;
also had to worry about preventing spam and abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:23.280
of the platform. To be honest, Twilio never&nbsp;
really figured out the solution to this issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.520
but luckily for Twilio, investors had. Back&nbsp;
in 2007, investors didn’t quite understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.920
value of Twilio but by the early 2010s, they&nbsp;
knew exactly what they were in for and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.760
were willing to back Twilio to the moon.&nbsp;
This included $12 million in late 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:48.400
$17 million in late 2011, $130 million in 2015,&nbsp;
and a $150 million IPO in 2016. This is really how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.040
Twilio addressed the infrastructure problem.&nbsp;
They just raised and spent a bunch of money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.080
but while that worked in the early days,&nbsp;
as Twilio becomes a mature company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:09.823
investors are looking for profitability and ROI&nbsp;
which brings us into Twilio’s current crisis.

00:09:09.823 --> 00:09:10.840
A BREAKING POINT:
The reality is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:17.160
Twilio is having an extremely rough time achieving&nbsp;
profitability. They’ve already tried doing layoffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:22.520
multiple times actually. Last September, they&nbsp;
laid off 11% of their staff and earlier this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:27.960
they laid of another 17% of their workforce. So,&nbsp;
within the past year, they’ve cut their workforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:33.840
by almost a third and they’re still burning&nbsp;
over a billion dollars per year. And the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:40.400
is that this situation is more a result of the&nbsp;
industry as opposed to Twilio themselves. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.880
the communications API industry has become&nbsp;
extremely cutthroat especially with Google and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:52.400
Amazon stepping into the arena. These juggernauts&nbsp;
view the industry in completely different light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:58.840
To them, the communications API industry is just&nbsp;
a small portion of one sector of one division of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:04.080
their business. They frankly don’t care about&nbsp;
this sector more than just controlling it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.600
telling their enterprise customers that they&nbsp;
offer these solutions as well. Aka, Google nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:16.160
Amazon really care about their SMS business being&nbsp;
profitable. For them, it’s all about the bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:21.800
picture. It’s about locking in Walmart or Ford as&nbsp;
their next massive cloud customer. If that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:28.360
that Walmart and Ford get subsidized OTPs, so be&nbsp;
it, it’s more than worth it. The same, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:34.360
cannot be said about Twilio who’s main business&nbsp;
itself is communications meaning that this itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:40.000
has to be profitable. And according to Twilio&nbsp;
themselves, they don’t expect GAAP profitability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.880
till 2027. With that being said, the good news is&nbsp;
that Twilio has a really strong pool of customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:52.840
including Uber, Airbnb, Instacart, Redit, and&nbsp;
Lyft just to name a few. And it’s unlikely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:57.720
these companies will move away from Twilio anytime&nbsp;
soon because Twilio has won over the developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:02.920
at these companies with their developer first&nbsp;
mindset. And, as long as Twilio has a strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:08.960
roster of customers it’s unlikely that investors&nbsp;
will stop supporting Twilio. Worst case scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:13.160
they have a couple of rough years until they&nbsp;
achieve profitability after which they’ll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:20.120
good to go. Also, Google and Amazon are by&nbsp;
no means trying to take down Twilio. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:25.280
they actually support Twilio. Just recently,&nbsp;
Twilio partnered with Google to create better AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:31.000
powered virtual customer support. And Amazon has&nbsp;
even gone as far as investing in Twilio buying up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:39.280
a 1% stake in the company. But, his could also be&nbsp;
perceived as bad news. You have a strong company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:45.080
with strong customers that’s facing a cash flow&nbsp;
problem. That makes them a perfect acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:50.520
target. Google and Amazon could easily put up the&nbsp;
$12 billion to buy the company. And they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:55.920
have a far easier time handling the expenses&nbsp;
with their in house infrastructure. They may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.520
even be able to achieve profitability with Twilio&nbsp;
but even if they don’t, it doesn’t really matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.440
because they would have a new list of customers&nbsp;
that they could sell Google Cloud or AWS to. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:11.400
the sake of Twilio and in hopes of preventing&nbsp;
big tech from controlling yet another industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:17.840
hopefully, Twilio is able to make it through on&nbsp;
their own but only time will tell. One fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:22.760
giant that’s seemingly beating the odds is&nbsp;
Nokia, check out this video to learn more.

